Paper Critic — Source-Code Audit and Professionalisation Plan
GitHub repository review · Original 14-page manuscript · Generated 8-page referee report · 21 November 2026

The repository is now accessible. I've verified that Kasi72/Paper-Critc is public and inspected the actual Kotlin implementation.

The original article you uploaded is also extremely useful: it allows us to identify not merely what is wrong with the generated report, but which parts of the application are responsible for those errors.
1. The three most consequential discoveries
1. The PDF extraction system is inadequate for rigorous journal appraisal.

The application attempts to extract text by manually scanning PDF byte streams and matching parenthesized text. It is not using a full PDF text-and-layout extraction engine. This can lose scientific text, symbols, equations, reading order and tables.



2. The AI frequently reviews an abbreviated version of the paper.

The application reduces the extracted manuscript to approximately 14,000 characters, retaining the beginning and end while discarding the middle. The middle of a scientific article often contains exactly the Methods and Results needed to validate its criticisms.



3. Verification and credibility scoring are not sufficiently independent.

The report's important judgments still depend heavily on model-generated prose. The code also permits the displayed credibility score and six-axis rubric to disagree.



These are implementation problems, not merely prompt-writing problems.
2. What the repository actually contains
The project is a native Android application built with Kotlin and Jetpack Compose, using Firebase AI Logic for its primary AI pipeline, with configurable external AI-provider support. It also includes local persistence, Firestore integration, a native PDF exporter and a multi-persona reviewer chat interface.

I found meaningful engineering work already present: structured response schemas, fallback model handling, a deterministic statistics module, manuscript-type classification, a red-team/blue-team pipeline and dedicated dossier formatting.

The architecture is not something I would discard. It needs substantial correction and separation of responsibilities.
