A genuine numerical inconsistency the app should have found
There is a much more interesting potential reporting error in the published article itself.

On page 8, the Results text gives a pooled long-stay bed estimate of 9, accompanied by a stated 95% CI of 1.26 to 2.35. That interval does not contain the point estimate of 9.

Yet Figure 2 on page 9 displays the long-stay pooled estimate as approximately 9 (95% CI 3–15).

That is a concrete cross-reference discrepancy worth flagging for verification. It may be a transcription or editorial error, but the application should not invent an explanation without checking the source and any correction notices. 
S0033291724002307a.pdf

What the improved reviewer should say:
Numerical consistency concern — Results versus Figure 2. The reported long-stay pooled point estimate of 9 is incompatible with the confidence interval of 1.26–2.35 given in the narrative. Figure 2 appears to give 3–15 instead. Verify the intended confidence limits and correct the discrepancy if confirmed.

This is a much stronger example of scientific reviewing than automatically recommending REML, Egger regression or publication-bias tests.

It is also a test of the whole architecture: the system needs reliable extraction of the Results text, visual or structured interpretation of Figure 2, numerical validation, and a linked finding.

5. The credibility score has a demonstrable code defect
Your report displays the following six-axis rubric:

02.557.510MethodologySignificanceClarityOriginalityMath rigorReproducibility









The six scores shown in the generated report

      



The weighted total is 63/100, but the headline says 72/100. 
need_estimates_of_psychiatric_beds_a_sys_referee_report.pdf

I traced the reason through two files:
- Reviewer2PipelineService.kt, lines 976–1017 calculates a weighted total but still permits the model's separate credibility score when it differs by up to 10 points. For larger discrepancies, it averages the two values rather than enforcing the weighted calculation.
- PaperReview.kt, lines 85–151 reconstructs individual rubric values by parsing text in editorNote, with fallback scores derived from the headline score.
This creates two loosely connected representations of the same assessment.

Exact correction: Store all six criterion scores as structured fields, calculate the composite exclusively from those fields, and render both the rubric and headline from the same immutable assessment object.

Do not reconstruct numerical values from an editorial paragraph.

The application should also distinguish a confidence score in the AI's extraction and verification from confidence in the scientific conclusions. Those are two different concepts.
