Additional code-level concerns
The quantitative heuristics are too aggressive. In QuantitativeForensicAnalyzer.kt, the heterogeneity check relies on detecting text patterns such as I² = .... When a statistic is inside a figure, expressed using a different inequality, or lost during PDF parsing, the system may incorrectly conclude that the analysis is missing. The same module can issue an Egger/publication-bias warning based primarily on overall study count, without first establishing whether the relevant synthesis supports such a test.

The prompts actively encourage over-criticism. In Reviewer2PipelineService.kt, the default review mode is AGGRESSIVE_REVIEWER2, and the prompt directs the model to assume zero benefit of the doubt. That may be useful for generating hypotheses, but it is poorly suited to deciding which hypotheses are established facts.

The two analytical passes are not properly reconciled. The statistical and editorial analyses run concurrently. Their results are assembled into the final review, but there is no subsequent, evidence-grounded adjudicator that must resolve contradictions between the statistical report and editorial conclusion.

External literature verification is separate from the main review. The Google Search grounding feature is implemented, but it is triggered separately from the dossier workflow in PaperCriticViewModel.kt. Consequently, the existence of that feature does not mean that every exported criticism has been independently checked against external information.

The JSON recovery mechanism is permissive. The pipeline tries to repair truncated model JSON by closing braces or cutting back to earlier properties. Combined with default values for missing fields, this can turn an incomplete response into a seemingly complete review. A professional system should distinguish successfully generated from successfully validated.

The present tests partly reinforce the wrong assumptions. The existing unit tests use a simplified psychiatric-bed manuscript and explicitly expect an unweighted-synthesis warning. They do not establish whether a warning remains appropriate after inspecting the complete published article. This is a strong reason to add full-document regression tests, not merely more isolated parser tests.
7. The redesigned scientific review engine
I recommend replacing the present orchestration with a verifiable evidence workflow, while preserving your red-team, blue-team, statistical and editorial capabilities.
01

Full-document ingestion

Capture all pages and preserve source coordinates; identify missing or unreadable content.




02

Research classification

Identify study type, primary question, research design and applicable appraisal instruments.




03

Factual evidence inventory

Extract study counts, methods, analyses, outcomes, tables, statistics and limitations.




04

Independent appraisal modules

Run design, statistics, reporting, reproducibility and applicability analyses.




05

Adversarial challenge

Generate candidate concerns, each anchored to the evidence inventory.




06

Counterevidence verification

Search the complete manuscript for evidence that disproves or qualifies every concern.




07

Final adjudication

Accept, qualify, dismiss or leave findings unverified; resolve contradictions.




08

Editorial synthesis

Write one coherent assessment from adjudicated findings only.




09

Report verification

Check calculations, citations, completeness, typography and PDF integrity.



The essential evidence data model
Instead of passing unstructured criticism between large prompts, create a structured finding object.

data class EvidenceAnchor(
    val documentId: String,
    val pageNumber: Int,
    val section: String?,
    val exactQuote: String,
    val startOffset: Int?,
    val endOffset: Int?,
    val boundingBox: List<Float>?
)

enum class FindingStatus {
    VERIFIED,
    PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED,
    CONTRADICTED,
    UNVERIFIED,
    NOT_APPLICABLE
}

data class ReviewFinding(
    val id: String,
    val title: String,
    val allegation: String,
    val severity: String,
    val status: FindingStatus,
    val supportingEvidence: List<EvidenceAnchor>,
    val counterEvidence: List<EvidenceAnchor>,
    val methodologicalJustification: String,
    val recommendedCorrection: String,
    val verificationNotes: String
)



The system should not permit a finding to be represented as verified unless the supporting evidence exists and the source coordinates have passed validation.

For claims about the absence of an analysis, the rule needs to be even stricter: verify that the relevant Methods, Results, tables, figures and available supplements were actually inspected. Failure to detect a term in extracted text is not evidence that the method was omitted.
8. Redesign the report around evidence, not agents
The current PDF has nine sections determined largely by the internal pipeline. This causes repetition and gives equal visual prominence to important findings and secondary AI-generated discussion.

I would replace it with two distinct documents:











A. Scientific Appraisal Report

The primary report for researchers, journal editors, clinicians and academic departments.

Target approximately 6–10 pages when justified by the manuscript. Emphasize the verdict, methodological quality, critical findings, uncertainty, applicability and required corrections.














B. Technical Verification Appendix

An optional detailed companion containing the complete evidence ledger, statistical checks, source passages, counterarguments, reproducibility observations and model limitations.

This is where your extensive 30-stage work belongs.



The distinction is important. A professional reviewer can be extremely thorough without forcing the reader through every intermediate analytical step.
Proposed primary report layout
Page or section	Content
Cover and executive summary	Verified article citation, review mode, core conclusion, strongest findings
Article profile	Research question, design, population, inclusion criteria, outcomes, study flow
Evidence map	Principal claims, supporting results and strength of evidence
Methodological appraisal	Design-specific strengths, limitations and reporting quality
Statistical audit	Verified errors, methods appropriateness, uncertainty and heterogeneity
Critical findings	Prioritized, source-linked findings with precise corrections
Clinical/policy relevance	Applicability, transferability, practical consequences and limitations
Final assessment	Balanced synthesis, confidence and outstanding verification tasks

For an already published article, the default should be Post-publication Critical Appraisal, not Minor Revision. A journal-referee mode can remain available when that is the user's purpose.
World-class PDF formatting standards
The code currently uses Android PdfDocument and manual Canvas positioning. This is a workable starting point, but it needs a document-layout system rather than a sequence of drawing commands.

I would establish the following standards:
- A4 with consistent margins and a proper typographic grid; approximately 10–11 pt body text and comfortable leading.
- A real serif/sans-serif type hierarchy, with tabular numerals for statistical values.
- Numbered findings with stable references, such as M-01 for methodology and Q-03 for quantitative issues.
- Source citations beside findings, with page references and clickable links where supported.
- Table row integrity, repeated column headers and controlled table continuation.
- Rules preventing section headings, severity cards and bullets from being orphaned at page boundaries.
- A proper final conclusion and evidence appendix rather than ending with proofreading notes.
- Embedded metadata, meaningful bookmarks, selectable text, accessibility tagging where supported, and hyperlink annotations.
Two exact layout defects are visible in the code.

First, PdfReportExporter.kt deliberately truncates rubric explanations to 106 characters. That is why several explanations on page 1 end with ellipses. Scientific content should never be silently cut to fit a decorative card.

Second, drawLayoutPaginated() splits text at the line level when space runs out. That explains why individual findings or paragraphs can break awkwardly across pages. A professional pagination engine should operate on paragraphs and semantic blocks, with widow/orphan rules and continuation logic.

I would retain the existing native PDF exporter as a fallback if offline export matters, but investigate a structured HTML/CSS print engine or a proper PDF-layout library for the high-quality report path. The choice should be based on Android compatibility, licensing, accessibility, and whether server-side rendering is acceptable.
9. Clinical and research-specific upgrades
Because this application will be used to scrutinize biomedical literature, it should offer an additional Clinical Evidence Appraisal mode.

That mode should evaluate more than statistical significance. It should ask whether patient selection is clinically representative, whether outcome measures matter, whether benefits and harms are balanced, whether the results transfer to resource-constrained settings, and what certainty is justified by the available evidence.

For example, the psychiatric-bed article presents internationally synthesized estimates but does not directly establish the correct capacity for any particular hospital or district. A useful clinical/policy appraisal would distinguish bed need estimates from observed utilization, accessibility, community alternatives, case mix, length of stay and actual capacity constraints.

I would also introduce design-specific appraisal pathways. PRISMA is relevant to this paper's reporting; an appropriate review-quality instrument is a separate matter. Do not treat reporting guidelines, risk-of-bias tools and certainty-of-evidence frameworks as interchangeable.
